{
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "Subject pronouns",
      "body": "These are the words for I, you, he, she, we, they. In Latin America, *ustedes* is the only plural \"you\" — used for any group, friends or strangers.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "",
          "Singular",
          "Plural"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "1st person",
            "*yo* — I",
            "*nosotros / nosotras* — we"
          ],
          [
            "2nd person (informal)",
            "*tú* — you",
            "*ustedes* — you all"
          ],
          [
            "2nd person (formal)",
            "*usted* — you",
            "*ustedes* — you all"
          ],
          [
            "3rd person",
            "*él* — he · *ella* — she",
            "*ellos / ellas* — they"
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Pronouns are usually left out",
      "body": "Because the verb ending already shows who is doing the action, Spanish usually drops the pronoun: *Soy de Chile* is more natural than *Yo soy de Chile*. Use the pronoun for emphasis or contrast (*Yo soy de Chile, y ella es de Perú*) or to make clear who you mean with *él*, *ella* and *usted*, which share the same verb form.\n\n*Nosotras* and *ellas* are for all-female groups; a mixed group uses *nosotros* and *ellos*. (In Spain you'll also hear *vosotros* for informal \"you all\" — you'll meet it later for recognition.)"
    },
    {
      "heading": "The verb ser (to be)",
      "body": "*Ser* is one of the two verbs for \"to be\". Use it to say who or what someone is, their nationality, where they're from, and their job.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
          "Pronoun",
          "ser",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          [
            "*yo*",
            "*soy*",
            "*Soy estudiante.* — I'm a student."
          ],
          [
            "*tú*",
            "*eres*",
            "*¿Eres de aquí?* — Are you from here?"
          ],
          [
            "*él / ella / usted*",
            "*es*",
            "*Ella es profesora.* — She's a teacher."
          ],
          [
            "*nosotros / nosotras*",
            "*somos*",
            "*Somos amigos.* — We're friends."
          ],
          [
            "*ustedes*",
            "*son*",
            "*¿Ustedes son de Perú?* — Are you all from Peru?"
          ],
          [
            "*ellos / ellas*",
            "*son*",
            "*Son mexicanos.* — They're Mexican."
          ]
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "heading": "Nationalities and origin",
      "body": "Say where you're from with *ser de* + place: *Soy de Canadá.* Say your nationality with *ser* + adjective: *Soy canadiense.* Nationalities are written with a small letter in Spanish.\n\nMany nationalities change for gender: *Él es mexicano, ella es mexicana.* Those ending in *-e* don't: *Él es canadiense, ella es canadiense.* For a man, *-és* nationalities become *-esa* for a woman: *inglés → inglesa*. Note there is no \"a/an\" before jobs: *Soy médico* (I'm a doctor)."
    }
  ],
